141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Our original plan was to use three different APIs for baseball-related data. First, we planned to scrape historical team performance data from Baseball Reference using BeautifulSoup. Second, we intended to use the MLB-StatsAPI  to collect how teams performed in the first two weeks of the current season. Third, we planned to use an MLB Data API to gather projected pitching and hitting statistics for individual players. The goal was to combine all of this data to calculate and visualize which MLB teams had the highest probability of winning their division, league, or the overall championship. Our calculations were going to use April performance trends and player projections to make these predictions.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (AFTER CONSIDERATION...)
/E (AFTER CONSIDERATION...)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (APIs and Websites + Data Gathering Goals...)
/E (APIs and Websites + Data Gathering Goals...)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
144 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Instead of working with baseball, we shifted our project to focus on professional golf. We found these APIS to be easier to use and we thought this data could be compiled together in a cleaner manner. We used three different APIs: SportsDataIO to pull finalized player tournament scores, the Sportradar API to get official world golf rankings, and the Live Golf Data API to get FedEx Cup rankings and player statistics like wins and top 10s. Our new goal became identifying the top players based on their performance in the current season and predicting who is most likely to win future events. We organized all of this data into a single SQLite database, calculated metrics such as average points per event, and built visualizations comparing players' finishes and projected likelihood of future success.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
